      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A transition from rest to motion implies the object's velocity changes from zero to non‑zero, i.e., it experiences acceleration.",
      "evidence": "Acceleration is defined as the rate of change of velocity; a change from 0 m/s to >0 m/s is acceleration.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Acceleration occurs only when the net external force on the object is non‑zero (the forces are unbalanced).",
      "evidence": "Newton’s second law, \\(F_{net}=ma\\), states that a non‑zero mass times a non‑zero acceleration requires a non‑zero net force.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore, the most likely reason the object begins to move is that an unbalanced force causes acceleration.",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 2 and 3: a change from rest requires acceleration, which requires an unbalanced force.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [3]
    }
  ],
